{%- doc -%}
  Outline icon for a wedding band style, to read at ~28px beside a menu link. The third of the
  set, after 'icon-ring-style' (how a setting arranges its stones) and 'icon-diamond-shape' (the
  cut of one stone).

  These draw the band, which the other two deliberately leave out: on an engagement setting the
  stones are what tell the styles apart, and on a wedding band the band is. So the ring is drawn
  face-on - outer edge and inner edge - and what varies is how much of it is set.

  Every stone is the same small circle on the same radius, so a column of these reads as one set
  and the difference between a half-set band and a fully set one is the only thing that moves.

  @param {string} style - one of wedding, anniversary, eternity, plain-metal
{%- enddoc -%}

<svg
  class="icon-band-style"
  viewBox="0 0 24 24"
  fill="none"
  stroke="currentColor"
  stroke-width="0.9"
  stroke-linejoin="round"
  stroke-linecap="round"
  aria-hidden="true"
  focusable="false"
>
  {%- case style -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The pair, not one band: "Wedding" is the whole collection rather than a style within it,
      and two bands overlapping is the one drawing here that says so without a stone on it.
      Smaller than the single-band icons below so the pair still fits the same box.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- when 'wedding' -%}
      <circle cx="8.6" cy="12" r="6.2"/>
      <circle cx="8.6" cy="12" r="4"/>
      <circle cx="15.4" cy="12" r="6.2"/>
      <circle cx="15.4" cy="12" r="4"/>

    {%- comment -%}
      Set across the front only - five stones over the upper half, which is how an anniversary
      band is made and what keeps it clear of the eternity band below it in the column.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- when 'anniversary' -%}
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9.6"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="6.4"/>
      <circle cx="6.86" cy="5.87" r="1.15"/>
      <circle cx="9.26" cy="4.48" r="1.15"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="4" r="1.15"/>
      <circle cx="14.74" cy="4.48" r="1.15"/>
      <circle cx="17.14" cy="5.87" r="1.15"/>

    {%- comment -%}
      All the way round, which is the whole of what the word means. Twelve stones at even spacing
      rather than as many as would fit: fewer reads as a half-set band turned, more closes up.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- when 'eternity' -%}
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9.6"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="6.4"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="4" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="16" cy="5.07" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="18.93" cy="8" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="20" cy="12" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="18.93" cy="16" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="16" cy="18.93" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="20" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="8" cy="18.93" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="5.07" cy="16" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="4" cy="12" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="5.07" cy="8" r="1.1"/>
      <circle cx="8" cy="5.07" r="1.1"/>

    {%- comment -%}
      No stone anywhere: the band on its own, at the same two radii the set bands are drawn on,
      so the four of them sit on one circle down the column.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- when 'plain-metal' -%}
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9.6"/>
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="6.4"/>
  {%- endcase -%}
</svg>
